>h2 Overview
>tree open Overview
CupidOS supports hardware x87 and SSE/SSE2 floating point in the kernel,
language, assembler, libm, and printf.

  - Target policy: kernel and Doom profiles use SSE/SSE2 floating code and
    16-byte call alignment. Native-oracle CFLAGS record this as
    `-mfpmath=sse -msse -msse2 -mstackrealign`.
  - Init: `fpu_init()` is the first call in `kmain`; every AP calls the
    no-log `fpu_init_cpu()` before any other C operation or compiler-generated
    SSE. That helper is built with target("general-regs-only") so its own
    setup cannot use floating registers. CR0 and CR4 are per-CPU: each path
    sets
    CR0.EM=0/MP=1/NE=1/TS=0 and
    CR4.OSFXSR=1/OSXMMEXCPT=1, runs FNINIT, and loads MXCSR=0x1F80.
  - Context switch: `context_switch.asm` FXSAVEs the outgoing PCB's
    `fp_state[512]` and FXRSTORs the incoming task's. Eager: no CR0.TS games.
  - PCB: `process_t.fp_state` is 512 bytes at 16-byte alignment. Offset 80.
  - Exceptions: IDT vectors 7 (#NM), 16 (#MF), 19 (#XF) route to
    `fpu_nm_handler`/`fpu_mf_handler`/`fpu_xf_handler`, which call `panic_fpu`
    with a serial dump of FSW/FCW/MXCSR. Should never fire under masked
    MXCSR + eager switch.
>endtree

>h2 CupidC FP Types
>tree open CupidC FP Types
  float:   4 bytes, SSE scalar.
  double:  8 bytes, SSE scalar.
  float4:  16 bytes, SSE packed (4 floats).
  double2: 16 bytes, SSE packed (2 doubles).

>h3 Shared Self-Hosting Path
The shared compiler used for self-hosting combines its i386 x87 transport and
arithmetic path with SSE conversion and comparison emission. It carries
non-atomic float and double values through objects, calls, variadic reads, and
returns. Explicit casts and assignment conversion work in either direction.
Matching floating conditional arms keep their width. Mixed arithmetic and
conditional arms use double.

The +=, -=, *=, and /= operators compute at the common width, convert the
stored result back to the left type, and evaluate the left designator once.
Each changed x87 result is stored at its C width before the next Linear IR
instruction.

Decimal constants, represented integer conversions, mixed integer-and-floating
arithmetic, and all six comparisons use the shared SSE path. A mixed floating
comparison uses double, and only != is true for an unordered NaN input.
Runtime float and double values convert to represented unsigned four-byte
targets across C's defined range. Binary32 widens exactly, and the result path
splits at 2^31 before signed truncation. ADR 0250 records this rule.

Non-atomic long double values use twelve-byte target objects. Automatic values
use frame snapshots. Static-duration scalars, fixed arrays, and complete
records may contain long-double leaves. Implicit initialization zeros the
complete object. An explicit leaf accepts a represented integer constant
expression or a bounded decimal L literal with parentheses and unary
signs. The emitter writes ten exact x87 value bytes, clears both padding
bytes, and selects .bss, .data, or .rodata from the payload and qualifiers.
Atomic leaves fail recursively without following pointers. Bounded finite normal
decimal L tokens round an exact ratio to a 64-bit explicit significand with
ties to even. The emitter writes that significand and the positive token's
biased exponent into a twelve-byte snapshot; unary minus supplies the sign.
The value-bearing ten bytes move through x87 80-bit FLD and FSTP memory forms in Cupid's shared x86 catalogue. The hosted
path converts among float, double, and long double, applies unary plus and
minus, and evaluates +, -, *, and /.
Source-head CupidASM and CupidDis also share signed x87 integer memory forms.
FILD and FISTP accept 16-bit, 32-bit, and 64-bit operands. Compiler head uses
the 64-bit forms for runtime conversion at every i386 integer width. It
presents input as signed 64-bit memory, adds exact 2^64 for an unsigned 64-bit
value with bit 63 set, and canonicalizes the 64-bit FISTP result to the target
width. The unsigned correction temporarily selects 64-bit x87 precision,
keeps the caller's rounding mode, and restores the complete saved control
word before the final store. Integer output saves and restores that word
around truncation toward zero. Static initializer conversion covers _Bool,
plain char, every signed or unsigned i386 integer width, and an enum whose
compatible integer type has the represented target layout. A nonzero integer
magnitude M with bit width w becomes x87 significand M << (64 - w). The high
word is (negative ? 0x8000 : 0) | (0x3fff + w - 1). Long-double input
truncates toward zero before its range check for integer destinations other
than _Bool.
_Bool tests the original floating value: both signed zeros become false, and
every represented finite nonzero value becomes true. The fixture converts
-0.5L to both targets, producing true for _Bool and zero for an unsigned
integer. Integer-valued zero keeps a CTOOL_C_INITIALIZER_ZERO record. The
checked seed does not carry this compiler path yet.
Direct and indirect fixed, variadic, and unprototyped arguments occupy twelve
cdecl bytes. Functions return the value in x87 ST0, and direct or indirect
callers store it in a twelve-byte snapshot. va_arg(long double) copies twelve
bytes and leaves the cursor at the following four-byte slot. All six
comparisons accept matching long-double values and mixed float or double
inputs. The emitter loads right then left, uses FUCOMIP ST0, ST1, and discards
the surviving x87 value. Signed zeros compare equal, and only != is true for
an unordered input. Hexadecimal or subnormal long-double literals, decimal
ratios beyond the bounded parser, static long-double arithmetic, comparison,
truth and logical operators, conditional selection, and width conversion
remain open.

The static scalar and aggregate proofs cover both scopes, mutable and const
objects, positive and negative values, both signed zeros, the largest accepted
bounded literal, exact section placement, symbols, padding, malformed
metadata, recovery, and deterministic repeated emission. The hosted i386
runtime reads all three target words for every literal payload. The conversion
fixture covers every integer kind, signed and unsigned enums, both signed
64-bit endpoints, ULLONG_MAX, and both the _Bool and unsigned results of
-0.5L. ADR 0251 records the static-data boundary, and ADR 0254 records
conversion.

The checked i386 Linux seed now carries static floating constant data and this
complete comparison path. The normal build uses them in kernel/gfx/jpeg.cc
and kernel/gfx/glyph_raster.cc. Their deterministic checked objects and guest
JPEG and glyph paths pass. ADR 0139 records the production transfer.

The checked seed understands the general-regs-only target on canonical
file-scope functions. It rejects compiler-generated floating instructions,
values, and call arguments while explicit source assembly keeps its separate
contract. It also accepts the exact ldmxcsr %0 memory input and
emits 0F AE 10 through the shared x86 model. The complete fpu_init_cpu body
passes unchanged. It also accepts the exact MOVSS float-memory round trip in
fpu_boot_smoke and its one-way load and store forms. Each form requires the
xmm0 clobber and emits F3 0F 10 00 or F3 0F 11 00 through EAX. The unchanged
stress_sin x87 block is also represented. It keeps one double output and one
double input, permits no clobbers, and emits FLD, FSIN, and FSTP through the
shared x86 model. The x87 stack returns to its original depth and no frame
temporary is used. Compiler head also accepts exact fldcw %0 with one
addressable, non-atomic 16-bit integer memory input. GNU semantics make the
no-output statement volatile even without that keyword. The emitter produces
D9 /5 through EAX. The checked seed does not carry this form yet.

The earlier compiler proof used kernel/cpu/fpu.c. The
unchanged implementation is now kernel/cpu/fpu.cc, a checked CupidC production
root. Two checked compiles produce the same validated 6,620-byte object with
SHA-256
14c3ea232b7d4455ceabd561c69293cc5849abae24d9f210aa69d64ed8c8a5cb.
The production object policy decodes fpu_init_cpu. It rejects helper calls
and floating work before the CR4 write, requires FNINIT before one 32-bit
memory LDMXCSR, and rejects any other floating work in the function.
Four-vCPU e1000 and RTL8139 boots print [fpu] SSE2 enabled,
[fpu] boot smoke ok, and FPU boot smoke passed before they finish
feature16_asm_fpu.cc successfully.

The checked seed also accepts the exact volatile EFLAGS restore in
simd_cpu_has_cpuid. One non-atomic 32-bit integer r input and one cc clobber
produce a checked POP EAX, PUSH EAX, POPF sequence. ESP remains balanced and
the object needs no relocation for this statement. Both unchanged restore
sites pass.

It also accepts the CPUID leaf input sharing EAX with the
compatible write-only output. It keeps the a and =a constraints, records
their match, and loads EAX immediately before CPUID. The checked seed emits
the six packed SSE2 statement shapes in kernel/cpu/simd.cc. Their ordered
inputs, memory effect, and XMM0 through XMM7 clobbers remain explicit. The
production wrapper freezes the source and its seven-header closure. Two
checked-seed builds produce the same validated 8,768-byte object with SHA-256
fd280c321b8eb38a90d4f0982d70b8df0364585e3da322eb2c9de722e071f8d4.
The normal SIMD recipe now uses this checked object.

The checked seed represents the exact x87 round-down statement in
str_floor. It takes one double memory output and one double memory input with
the exact ax and memory clobbers. Emission saves the incoming x87 control word
below ESP, selects round toward negative infinity, executes FRNDINT, restores
the saved word, and stores the result. It reuses the consumed input-address
slot for scratch, leaves the pending output address intact, and needs no frame
temporary.

A bounded shared-decoder oracle checks eight binary64 inputs under all four
incoming rounding modes. It verifies exact results, balanced x87 depth, and
control-word restoration without executing native x87 code. Two exact
compiles of the unchanged helper produce the same 420-byte object.

The checked seed also emits the later explicit non-atomic double-to-uint64_t
casts. It derives the high word from value / 2^32, subtracts that exact
multiple, and derives the low word from the remainder. Each unsigned-word
step splits at 2^31 before signed SSE truncation. The decoder-driven oracle
covers positive and negative fractions and the active range through the
largest binary64 value below 2^64.
Full unchanged kernel/core/string.cc compiles twice to the same 14,460-byte
object with SHA-256
d48bb6ea18b7124fbefeaca0d5d5ee8a517db950f21ea88e30ededd6c5c2a577.
The production wrapper freezes the source and its two headers, validates the
ELF32 object, and publishes it without a host compiler. ADR 0181 records the
transfer.

The checked seed also represents the x87 power statements in libm_pow_impl and
libm_powf_impl. The double form has five double memory operands. The mixed
form has a float output, two float inputs, and two double inputs. Both require
one memory clobber. Linear IR evaluates each set of five addresses once in
source order. Each 116-byte focused function contains seventeen x87
instructions, uses DC E9 for FSUB ST(1), ST(0), reaches stack depth three,
and returns to its incoming depth without a relocation. All seven active
range-reduction sites use the corrected GNU spelling for x - round(x). The
old DC E1 spelling remains a compatibility contract. ADRs 0208 and 0209
record seed carriage and the runtime-tested source correction.

The checked seed also represents the exact volatile sqrtsd %1, %0 statement in
libm_sqrt_impl. It accepts one modifiable, non-atomic double =x output, one
non-atomic double x input, and no clobbers. Linear IR evaluates the output
address before the input value. The 65-byte focused function uses the shared
MOVSD and SQRTSD encodings and has no relocations.

The checked seed also represents the exact volatile x87 statement in
libm_atan2_impl. It accepts one modifiable, non-atomic double =m output, two
addressable, non-atomic double m inputs in y, x order, and one memory
clobber. Linear IR evaluates all three addresses once in source order. The
53-byte focused function uses the shared model for both loads, FPATAN, and
the final store, with no relocations.

The checked seed also represents the exact volatile x87 statement in
libm_exp_impl. It accepts one modifiable, non-atomic double =m output, two
addressable, non-atomic double m inputs in x, log2e order, and one memory
clobber. Linear IR evaluates all three addresses once in source order. The
71-byte focused function has no relocations, reaches x87 depth three, and
returns to its incoming depth.

The checked seed also represents the exact aligned fabs mask block and the
following fabs and fabsf wrappers. The masks occupy the first 32 bytes of
.rodata, with local labels at offsets 0 and 16. The double wrapper uses MOVSD
and ANDPD in 15 text bytes. The float wrapper uses MOVSS and ANDPS in 14
bytes. Each has one absolute relocation to its mask.

The following floor, floorf, ceil, ceilf, round, roundf, trunc, and truncf
wrappers are represented by the checked seed too. They save the incoming x87
control word, select down, up, nearest-even, or toward-zero mode, apply
FRNDINT, and restore the original word. Together they occupy 384 text bytes
without relocations, reach x87 depth one, and balance ESP and x87 depth.

The following fmod and fmodf wrappers are represented by the checked seed too.
Each repeats FPREM while x87 status-word C2 is set, uses a short backward
branch, discards the divisor, and returns the remainder through XMM0. Each
body contains 35 text bytes, reaches x87 depth two, balances ESP and x87
depth, and has no relocation.

The checked seed also represents the aligned libm_log2e_const and libm_ln2_const
data and the next eight exponent and logarithm wrappers. The constants occupy
16 read-only bytes at alignment eight. exp2 and exp2f use FRNDINT, F2XM1, and
FSCALE. exp and expf multiply by log2(e) first. log2 and log2f use FLD1 with
FYL2X, while log and logf use ln(2). The functions add 264 text bytes and
four absolute relocations, never exceed x87 depth three, and balance ESP and
x87 depth. The source then reaches pow on line 846.

The checked seed represents that wrapper and all 17 later cdecl bridges.
The pow, hypot, and nextafter pairs take two arguments. The asin, acos, sinh,
cosh, tanh, and cbrt pairs take one. Each wrapper copies its original cdecl
words, calls a matching external implementation, reclaims the copied words,
and moves the ST(0) result into XMM0 at float or double width. The family
adds 558 text bytes and 18 PC-relative call relocations. Corrected
kernel/cpu/libm.cc emits a deterministic 16,164-byte ELF32 object through
the checked production wrapper. The wrapper freezes kernel/core/types.h and
kernel/cpu/libm.h with the source. The guest smoke runs
/bin/feature15_libm.cc and requires the seven-case x87 summary, all 29 checks,
and PASS feature15_libm. ADR 0209 records the numerical correction.

Runtime float, double, and automatic long double values work with unary !,
&&, ||, the controlling operand of ?:, the conditions of if, while, do, and
for, and conversion to _Bool. Both signed zeros are false; finite nonzero
values, subnormals, infinities, and NaNs are true. Increment and decrement,
hexadecimal floating constants, binary32 and binary64 subnormal constants,
hexadecimal or subnormal long-double literals, decimal ratios beyond the
bounded parser, static long-double arithmetic, comparison, truth and logical
operators, conditional selection, floating-width conversion, general SIMD
value semantics, and atomic floating access remain outside this shared path.
Twelve-byte direct and
indirect fixed, variadic, and unprototyped arguments, function returns, direct
and indirect call results, and va_arg(long double) use the represented
automatic long-double path. The exact production SIMD assembly forms above
have their own checked path. The SSE details below describe the private in-OS
compiler.

The private compiler passes represented scalar and pointer values in
four-byte cdecl slots and double values in eight-byte slots. Direct,
function-pointer, and method calls preserve left-to-right evaluation, arrange
complete words in source order, and use the same widths for callee parameter
offsets and caller cleanup. The feature13 guest exercises ten calls through
one double, double, double, int helper.

A direct function or method with parsed fixed parameter types converts
represented integer, char, float, and double arguments to the declared slot
type before cdecl layout. Represented pointer categories and integer null forms
can fill a pointer slot. A represented object pointer can fill a fixed int or
unsigned int slot as one unchanged i386 word. Narrow and floating destinations
remain rejected. A parsed variadic tail widens float to double and
promotes char to int. Function-pointer calls, kernel bindings, and calls
without that metadata keep their source-width slots.

Private decimal float and double literals use a fixed 1536-bit integer
workspace. CupidC forms the exact decimal ratio and rounds once to the selected
IEEE width with ties going to even. An f suffix selects binary32 before
rounding. The converter covers subnormals, finite limits, infinity, and signed
zero. Numeric tokens may contain 95 characters including their suffix.
Hexadecimal floating and long double literals remain unsupported.

Fixed float and double arrays keep their element type through one, two, or
three dimensions in global, automatic, block-static, and persistent REPL
storage. The private compiler uses the remaining row stride for each
subscript and typed SSE access at the leaf. sizeof(array[index]) reports the
row without running the index. Depth-one floating pointers retain their width
through address expressions, returns, function and method array parameters,
dereference, subscripts, scaled direct pointer updates, and assignment.
Structure and class objects, arrays, and pointers keep scalar floating fields
and one-dimensional fixed floating field arrays. Bounds and dimension products
are checked before allocation. Deeper floating pointers, indirect floating
updates, pointer-to-array types, and assignment through a
pointer-valued floating field subscript remain unsupported.

One-dimensional fixed float4 and double2 arrays use 16-byte elements in
global, automatic, block-static, and persistent REPL storage. Indexed access
uses unaligned-safe packed moves. Plain assignment and +=, -=, *=, and /=
retain the vector type and allow lane extraction. Matching vectors support
direct +, -, *, and /. Every direct operator keeps the written left value in
the machine destination. MIN and MAX intrinsics keep the second input for NaN
and equal signed-zero cases. A both-NaN ADD or MUL may carry either input
payload, depending on the processor or emulator. SIMD pointers,
multidimensional arrays, record fields, new, array parameters, and call ABI
transport remain unsupported.

>h3 Arithmetic
Binary operators `+`, `-`, `*`, `/` work on scalar float/double via SSE
scalar opcodes (ADDSS/ADDSD/etc.). Implicit promotion:
  `int + float -> float`, `char + int -> int`,
  `char + float -> float`, `float + double -> double`.
SIMD types need matching types for `+/-/*//`; mixing scalar and SIMD is a
compile error.

>h3 Casts
`(int)3.7` -> 3 (truncating). `(float)5` -> 5.0. A char uses the same
integer-to-floating conversion path. `(double)1.5f` widens. Casts lower to
CVTSI2SS/CVTTSS2SI/CVTSS2SD/etc.

>h3 Element Access
`v.x / v.y / v.z / v.w` on float4 extracts a scalar float via SHUFPS.
`v.x / v.y` on double2 via SHUFPD.

>h3 Intrinsics
`_mm_*_ps` (17 variants) and `_mm_*_pd` (11 variants) are recognized by name
and inlined as SSE opcodes. See `kernel/cpu/simd_intrin.h` for the full list.
>endtree

>h2 libm
>tree open libm
25 operations (50 symbols with f-variants).

Hardware fast-paths:
  sqrt, sin, cos, tan, atan, atan2, fabs, floor, ceil, round,
  trunc, fmod, exp2, log2.

Composite:
  exp, log, pow, asin, acos, sinh, cosh, tanh, cbrt, hypot, nextafter.

>box
libm functions use a CupidC-internal ABI and return results in XMM0 instead of
ST(0), as System V expects. Default GCC code in the kernel cannot call these
functions directly. CupidC JIT code reaches them through the BIND_T table.
>endbox
>endtree

>h2 CupidASM Opcodes
>tree open CupidASM Opcodes
CupidASM supports about 80 FPU and SSE opcodes in these groups:

  FPU state control (FXSAVE, FXRSTOR, FINIT, FNINIT, FWAIT, LDMXCSR, STMXCSR)
  SSE scalar  (23): MOVSS/SD, ADDSS/SD, and more.
  SSE packed  (24): MOVAPS/UPS, ADDPS/PD, SHUFPS, CMPPS, and more.
  x87         (26): FLD, FUCOMIP, FSIN, FPATAN, F2XM1, FYL2X, and more.

Register tokens: XMM0-7 and ST0-7.
>endtree

>h2 Testing
>tree open Testing
Test programs:
  `bin/feature12_float.cc`    scalar float arithmetic, casts, element access.
  `bin/feature13_double.cc`   decimal payloads, typed lvalues, calls, and libm.
  `bin/feature14_simd.cc`     packed operators, arrays, NaNs, and intrinsics.
  `bin/feature15_libm.cc`     29 fixed checks, including seven x87 range paths.
  `bin/feature16_asm_fpu.cc`  CupidC inline asm using SSE + x87.
  `bin/fp_drill.cc`           manual #XF provocation (panics kernel).
  `demos/fpu_kernel.asm`      CupidASM FPU state + scalar + packed + x87.
  `demos/simd_blur.asm`       SIMD box blur via MOVUPS/ADDPS/MULPS.

Interactive: boot QEMU graphical, type the command in the shell.

[Run float feature test](shell:cc /bin/feature12_float.cc)
>endtree

>h2 Stress Test
>tree open Stress Test
fpu_context_stress() is defined in kernel/cpu/fpu.cc, but the normal boot does
not call it. The checked CupidC production recipe does not consume host
CFLAGS, so the old -DFPU_STRESS instruction is no longer a valid activation
path.
Panics on corruption.
>endtree

>h2 Exception Drill
>tree open Exception Drill
`fp_drill` shell command unmasks DE in MXCSR and divides by zero, verifying
the #XF handler panics with MXCSR dump.

>box
Running `fp_drill` requires a kernel reboot afterward.
>endbox
>endtree

>h2 Verification
>box
How to verify P1 FPU/SSE:

cc /bin/feature12_float.cc

Expected output: PASS

[Run now](shell:cc /bin/feature12_float.cc)
>endbox

